    "current_example": {
      "math_academy_id": "e-6930",
      "problem": "If $f'(x)=|x+1|,$ sketch a possible graph of a continuous function $y=f(x).$",
      "worked_solution": "The graph of $y=f'(x)$ is the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14200695/assets/57937f50c8adac57cd0a8b131ae2605466fe5e87e85ba324977a95b2f2c7a006.png)\n\nRecall that critical points of $y=f(x)$ occur when $f'(x)=0$ or $f'(x)$ does not exist. In our case, there is one critical point: \n\n$\\qquad$ $x=-1$ \n\nAlso, we have the following: \n\n- If $f'(x) > 0$ then $f(x)$ is increasing.\n\n- If $f'(x) <      0$ then $f(x)$ is decreasing.\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty,-1)$ | $-1$ | $(-1,\\infty)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f'$ | $+$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n| $f$ | $\\nearrow$ | $\\textrm{no extr.}$ | $\\nearrow$ |\n\nLet's now sketch the graph of $y=f(x)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n- First, we draw the critical point. We choose an arbitrary point on the vertical line $x=-1,$ as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14200695/assets/296d0c0e63bedc37841aa914e0fb07320fff634ea1f6ac019a84cc47b87966b4.png)\n\n- Next, we consider the behavior of $y=f(x)$ in the neighborhood of the critical points:\n\n- Since $f(x)$ is increasing $\\nearrow$ on $(-\\infty,-1)$ and on $(-1,\\infty)$, the graph of $y=f(x)$ goes down the left of $x=-1$ and goes up to the right of $x=-1.$\n\n- Furthermore, since $f'(x)$ is decreasing to the left of $x = -1$, we must have $f''(x) <      0$, and, since it is increasing to the right of $x = -1$, we must have $f''(x) >    0.$ So, $x =-1$ is an inflection point at which the curve $y=f(x)$ changes from concave down to concave up.\n\nSo, we can draw as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14200695/assets/1c4fb55a07e1b55448ec2a2ada4fea07f363542cdf04a8676c90938b5c4f7fbe.png)\n\n- Finally, since we are told that our graph must be continuous, by connecting and extending the existing parts with a smooth curve, we obtain a possible graph of $y=f(x)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14200695/assets/dfeb793809943dc496cc1f47b26f9b20aa3403b6d999787a7444ede605547226.png)",
      "html_path": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14200695/observations/step-e6930-5b8c959dc642c019abd9.html",
